{
  "kind": "tea",
  "teaId": "hannah-prayer",
  "text": "She prayed so hard the priest thought she was drunk and told her to sober up.\n\nElkanah has two wives. Peninnah has kids. Hannah doesn't. And Scripture calls Peninnah her adversary, and says she provoked her sore, year after year, every single time they went up to worship.\n\nAt the one place she goes to meet God.\n\nSo Hannah stops eating and weeps. And her husband, who genuinely loves her, says the most well-meaning wrong thing in the Bible: \"Am not I better to thee than ten sons?\"\n\nSir. Read the room.\n\nSo she gets up from the table and goes to the house of the Lord. And she prays in bitterness of soul.\n\nHere's the detail. She spake in her heart. Only her lips moved. Her voice was not heard.\n\nAnd Eli the priest, whose actual job is recognising devotion, watches her mouth and decides she's drunk. Out loud. \"How long wilt thou be drunken? Put away thy wine from thee.\"\n\nAnd Hannah does not shrink and does not fight. She says, \"No, my lord. I am a woman of a sorrowful spirit. I have poured out my soul before the Lord.\"\n\nThe most honest prayer in that building. And the priest called her a drunk.\n\nShe walks out still childless. And the text says her countenance was no more sad.",
